\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Loss densities and model comparison results over the evaluation period March 1st 2016 to March 1st 2026. Panels a through c show the KDE plots of the forecast losses of all 60 fitted models over the evaluation period for each dataset. The KDE plots are drawn separately for each group of models based on their likelihood function. There are overall three distinct likelihood functions used in the construction of the models, the Gaussian, the Student's t and the Standardized Skew Student's t likelihood functions. The average forecast loss of the benchmark model and the model with the minimum average loss (denoted minimum QLIKE model) are shown on the plots by vertical lines. Panels d through f on the right show the scatter plots of the average forecast losses along the y-axis for each of the 60 models indexed by the model number on the x-axis. The benchmark Gaussian GARCH(1,1) model is marked by an asterisk (*) in each plot and the outperforming models, if any, are indicated by orange squares. Models that do not outperform the benchmark are indicated by blue dots.}}{18}{figure.caption.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:kdes_1}{{1}{18}{Loss densities and model comparison results over the evaluation period March 1st 2016 to March 1st 2026. Panels a through c show the KDE plots of the forecast losses of all 60 fitted models over the evaluation period for each dataset. The KDE plots are drawn separately for each group of models based on their likelihood function. There are overall three distinct likelihood functions used in the construction of the models, the Gaussian, the Student's t and the Standardized Skew Student's t likelihood functions. The average forecast loss of the benchmark model and the model with the minimum average loss (denoted minimum QLIKE model) are shown on the plots by vertical lines. Panels d through f on the right show the scatter plots of the average forecast losses along the y-axis for each of the 60 models indexed by the model number on the x-axis. The benchmark Gaussian GARCH(1,1) model is marked by an asterisk (*) in each plot and the outperforming models, if any, are indicated by orange squares. Models that do not outperform the benchmark are indicated by blue dots}{figure.caption.3}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Loss densities and model comparison results over the evaluation period March 1st 2000 to March 1st 2010. Panels a through c show the KDE plots of the forecast losses of all 60 fitted models over the evaluation period for each dataset. The KDE plots are drawn separately for each group of models based on their likelihood function. There are overall three distinct likelihood functions used in the construction of the models, the Gaussian, the Student's t and the Standardized Skew Student's t likelihood functions. The average forecast loss of the benchmark model and the model with the minimum average loss (denoted minimum QLIKE model) are shown on the plots by vertical lines. Panels d through f on the right show the scatter plots of the average forecast losses along the y-axis for each of the 60 models indexed by the model number on the x-axis. The benchmark Gaussian GARCH(1,1) model is marked by an asterisk (*) in each plot and the outperforming models, if any, are indicated by orange squares. Models that do not outperform the benchmark are indicated by blue dots.}}{19}{figure.caption.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:kdes_2}{{2}{19}{Loss densities and model comparison results over the evaluation period March 1st 2000 to March 1st 2010. Panels a through c show the KDE plots of the forecast losses of all 60 fitted models over the evaluation period for each dataset. The KDE plots are drawn separately for each group of models based on their likelihood function. There are overall three distinct likelihood functions used in the construction of the models, the Gaussian, the Student's t and the Standardized Skew Student's t likelihood functions. The average forecast loss of the benchmark model and the model with the minimum average loss (denoted minimum QLIKE model) are shown on the plots by vertical lines. Panels d through f on the right show the scatter plots of the average forecast losses along the y-axis for each of the 60 models indexed by the model number on the x-axis. The benchmark Gaussian GARCH(1,1) model is marked by an asterisk (*) in each plot and the outperforming models, if any, are indicated by orange squares. Models that do not outperform the benchmark are indicated by blue dots}{figure.caption.4}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Scatter plots of average model forecast losses. The panels on the left show the scatter plots over the validation period spanning from March 1st 2016 through March 1st 2026, and the panels on the left show the same plots for the same datasets over the period March 1st 2000 to March 1st 2010. The average forecast losses of models that are included in the model confidence set are indicated with orange squares. Models that are excluded from the model confidence set have their average forecast losses indicated by blue dots in the plots.}}{20}{figure.caption.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:mcs_all}{{3}{20}{Scatter plots of average model forecast losses. The panels on the left show the scatter plots over the validation period spanning from March 1st 2016 through March 1st 2026, and the panels on the left show the same plots for the same datasets over the period March 1st 2000 to March 1st 2010. The average forecast losses of models that are included in the model confidence set are indicated with orange squares. Models that are excluded from the model confidence set have their average forecast losses indicated by blue dots in the plots}{figure.caption.5}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Loss densities and model comparison results over the evaluation period March 1st 2016 to March 1st 2026 for 4-step ahead conditional forecasts. Panels a through c show the KDE plots of the forecast losses of all 60 fitted models over the evaluation period for each dataset. The KDE plots are drawn separately for each group of models based on their likelihood function. There are overall three distinct likelihood functions used in the construction of the models, the Gaussian, the Student's t and the Standardized Skew Student's t likelihood functions. The average forecast loss of the benchmark model and the model with the minimum average loss (denoted minimum QLIKE model) are shown on the plots by vertical lines. Panels d through f on the right show the scatter plots of the average forecast losses along the y-axis for each of the 60 models indexed by the model number on the x-axis. The benchmark Gaussian GARCH(1,1) model is marked by an asterisk (*) in each plot and the outperforming models, if any, are indicated by orange squares. Models that do not outperform the benchmark are indicated by blue dots.}}{21}{figure.caption.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:kdes_4}{{4}{21}{Loss densities and model comparison results over the evaluation period March 1st 2016 to March 1st 2026 for 4-step ahead conditional forecasts. Panels a through c show the KDE plots of the forecast losses of all 60 fitted models over the evaluation period for each dataset. The KDE plots are drawn separately for each group of models based on their likelihood function. There are overall three distinct likelihood functions used in the construction of the models, the Gaussian, the Student's t and the Standardized Skew Student's t likelihood functions. The average forecast loss of the benchmark model and the model with the minimum average loss (denoted minimum QLIKE model) are shown on the plots by vertical lines. Panels d through f on the right show the scatter plots of the average forecast losses along the y-axis for each of the 60 models indexed by the model number on the x-axis. The benchmark Gaussian GARCH(1,1) model is marked by an asterisk (*) in each plot and the outperforming models, if any, are indicated by orange squares. Models that do not outperform the benchmark are indicated by blue dots}{figure.caption.6}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Loss densities and model comparison results over the evaluation period March 1st 2016 to March 1st 2026 for 7-step ahead conditional forecasts. Panels a through c show the KDE plots of the forecast losses of all 60 fitted models over the evaluation period for each dataset. The KDE plots are drawn separately for each group of models based on their likelihood function. There are overall three distinct likelihood functions used in the construction of the models, the Gaussian, the Student's t and the Standardized Skew Student's t likelihood functions. The average forecast loss of the benchmark model and the model with the minimum average loss (denoted minimum QLIKE model) are shown on the plots by vertical lines. Panels d through f on the right show the scatter plots of the average forecast losses along the y-axis for each of the 60 models indexed by the model number on the x-axis. The benchmark Gaussian GARCH(1,1) model is marked by an asterisk (*) in each plot and the outperforming models, if any, are indicated by orange squares. Models that do not outperform the benchmark are indicated by blue dots.}}{22}{figure.caption.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:kdes_7}{{5}{22}{Loss densities and model comparison results over the evaluation period March 1st 2016 to March 1st 2026 for 7-step ahead conditional forecasts. Panels a through c show the KDE plots of the forecast losses of all 60 fitted models over the evaluation period for each dataset. The KDE plots are drawn separately for each group of models based on their likelihood function. There are overall three distinct likelihood functions used in the construction of the models, the Gaussian, the Student's t and the Standardized Skew Student's t likelihood functions. The average forecast loss of the benchmark model and the model with the minimum average loss (denoted minimum QLIKE model) are shown on the plots by vertical lines. Panels d through f on the right show the scatter plots of the average forecast losses along the y-axis for each of the 60 models indexed by the model number on the x-axis. The benchmark Gaussian GARCH(1,1) model is marked by an asterisk (*) in each plot and the outperforming models, if any, are indicated by orange squares. Models that do not outperform the benchmark are indicated by blue dots}{figure.caption.7}{}}
